  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.4 x² + 0.4 z²)/2, drawn at half its value as a surface. Steps shrink the x error by the factor 1 − 2.4 × rate, so x swings grow once the rate is above 0.833. This happens near the top of the slider range.",
    "Each step is −rate × (slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian, centred on the true slope, with spread sqrt((64 − b)/(63 b)) for batch size b, so it is exactly zero at 64. It comes from a fixed seed: Reset replays it, and a new run uses the next values.",
    "Both arrows are steps drawn at 3 times their true length on the same scale. Purple is the next mini-batch step (where the ball goes). Dark is the step the full data would give. A noisy step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "A run is finished after 40 steps. It is diverged when the loss goes above 5. A point off the drawn bowl is shown at the rim, and the Loss readout keeps its true value.",
    "Running, the ball takes 5 steps per second at 1× speed. The trail holds the 40 landing points of this run, and the ghost holds the previous run's points."
  ]
}